**2. 2025 projection from 2024 values at 3.4%**

Inferred rounding rule: 2023→2024 values are whole-dollar figures and the published 2024 values end in 0 or 5 at tier level; I infer that the published 2024 tier value was rounded to the dollar (nearest dollar), and that the 3.4% CPI index change is applied to the unrounded 2024 figure then rounded to the nearest dollar for publication. Because I do not have unrounded 2024 values, I apply the 3.4% to the published 2024 values and then round to the nearest dollar. I note this is a necessary inference from the data given.

Projection = round(2024 × 1.034) to nearest dollar.

- Tier A: 130,461 × 1.034 = 134,897.474 → $134,897 (rounded up)
- Tier B: 3,914 × 1.034 = 4,047.276 → $4,047 (rounded up)
- Tier C: 26,092 × 1.034 = 26,979.128 → $26,979 (rounded up)
- Tier D: 1,305 × 1.034 = 1,349.37 → $1,349 (rounded up)

If the inferred rule were “round 3.4% to one decimal, i.e., 1.034, applied to a final value rounded to the nearest dollar”, the numbers above are the mechanical result.

If instead the official practice rounds the percentage to an integer cent or applies a different rounding direction, results shift by a few dollars. Given the 2025 actual tier A is $134,841, this discrepancy is confirmed below.

